+# Security audit and policy rollout
+
+## Scope and findings
+
+This audit covers the dependency-free Node HTTP server and the Daily Grind static page. No browser verification was performed. The repository contains no authentication, sessions, database, payment processing, or working account APIs. The account and ordering links currently return the same page. `/logout` is a placeholder response; it does not invalidate a session. Do not present those flows as functional authentication or commerce. If sessions are added, implement server-side invalidation, Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite cookies, and CSRF-protected state-changing requests before launch.
+
+| Finding | Preventative measure |
+| --- | --- |
+| No content policy or framing protection | CSP observation/enforcement modes; immediate `object-src`, `base-uri`, and `frame-ancestors` restrictions; `X-Frame-Options: DENY` |
+| Inline styling makes a restrictive policy harder to deploy | Styles moved into `/styles.css`; no inline style exception required |
+| Missing browser security headers | `nosniff`, explicit referrer and permissions policies, and `no-store` on all application responses |
+| Request URL parsing depended on untrusted Host and could throw | Fixed parsing origin, guarded parsing, rejection of absolute/network-path targets |
+| Arbitrary methods accepted by page routes | GET/HEAD only; report collection accepts only POST; 405 responses include Allow |
+| No policy deployment telemetry | Validated `/csp-report` receiver with a 16 KiB limit, 60 requests/minute per process, and restricted structured logs |
+| Unbounded default connection lifetime | Explicit request/header/keep-alive timeouts and maximum requests per socket |
+
+The server reads only a fixed asset map and the page; URL paths never select a filesystem file. Hidden path segments return 404. Existing public route fallbacks remain available. No third-party runtime packages were introduced.
+
+## Deploy and observe
+
+1. Run `npm test` and `npm run build`. Use a maintained Node.js LTS release.
+2. Terminate HTTPS at a trusted reverse proxy. Keep the Node listener private behind that proxy and apply connection/body/rate limits there, including for `/csp-report`. The in-process report limit bounds telemetry volume; it is not a distributed denial-of-service defense.
+3. Start with `CSP_MODE=report-only npm start` (the default). The full policy is sent in `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`. A separate enforced policy already prohibits frames, objects, and base URL changes.
+4. Collect stderr in your deployment logging system. Reports produce JSON records such as `{"event":"csp-violation","directive":"style-src-elem","mode":"report-only"}`. Reports deliberately omit URLs, query strings, cookies, and source samples. They are untrusted, may be forged or missing, and are not proof of compromise. A report-only policy observes violations; it does not block the corresponding loads.
+5. Review representative production traffic, including navigation and the hero image, for unexpected violations. The only permitted external resource origin is `https://images.unsplash.com`, which hosts the existing stock photograph. Add an origin only after establishing why the app needs it. Do not add wildcard hosts, `unsafe-inline`, or `unsafe-eval` as a shortcut.
+6. Promote with `CSP_MODE=enforce npm start` and restart the process. The same full policy now uses the enforced CSP header and continues reporting. Monitor availability, asset loading, and report counts.
+7. To roll back a policy compatibility issue, restore `CSP_MODE=report-only` and restart. `Cache-Control: no-store` prevents new responses from being cached; already open documents retain their policy until reloaded. Invalid CSP_MODE values stop startup rather than silently disabling protection.
+
+The CSP report receiver uses the broadly supported `report-uri` mechanism with `application/csp-report` or `application/json` bodies. It does not advertise a Reporting API endpoint or support Reporting API report batches. Oversized bodies return 413, malformed reports 400, unsupported media types 415, and excess reports 429. Logs contain only directive and deployment mode, so investigation may require a controlled client-side reproduction without collecting sensitive production URLs.
+
+## HTTPS and remaining deployment responsibilities
+
+Configure HTTP-to-HTTPS redirects and HSTS at the TLS terminator, on HTTPS responses only. Begin with a short HSTS lifetime and extend it once HTTPS is reliable. Include subdomains or request preload only after verifying every affected host supports HTTPS. The application does not infer transport security from spoofable `X-Forwarded-Proto` headers and does not send HSTS from its plain HTTP listener. CSP intentionally does not upgrade local HTTP connections.
+
+Keep deployment secrets outside the repository, restrict log access and retention, and avoid exposing the application source directory through a separate static file server. Revisit the policy when scripts, forms, APIs, fonts, authentication, or third-party integrations are introduced. This audit does not establish the security of hosting infrastructure or services absent from this repository.
+
+## Automated validation
+
+`npm test` covers headers across page/error/report routes, enforcement configuration, local CSS and permitted image origin, inline-content removal, HEAD behavior, method restrictions, Host-independent parsing, hidden-file protection, report validation, body and rate limits, and log privacy. `npm run build` checks server syntax. These checks do not execute a browser's CSP engine or confirm production proxy configuration.
+
+References: [MDN CSP guide](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP), [MDN report-only header](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only), [MDN HSTS](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Strict-Transport-Security).
